  <AboutText>k__BackingField: '<b><size=+10>Overview</size></b>

    Clock Solitaire
    is a simple game which entirely comes down to the luck of the draw. It is played
    with a standard 52-card deck arranged into 13 piles, with one pile in the centre
    surrounded by 12 piles in a circle.


    The goal of Clock is to turn every
    card face up with the final card revealed being a King. This is done by moving
    the current face-up card to the bottom of its designated pile and then turning
    the top card face up and repeating the process. The game ends when the 4th King
    is turned face-up and victory is achieved if all 52 cards are face-up when this
    happens.


    <b><size=+10>History</size></b>

    Clock has one of the longest
    histories of Solitaire games that we have documented. Known initially as The
    Clock, its rules are documented in the 1869 book "Patience: A Series of Games
    with Cards" written by Ednah Dow Cheney. The game has remained unchanged since
    this publication.


    As with many old card games, Clock likely finds its true
    origins among cartomancers (fortune tellers). Due to the game''s nature of not
    depending on human ability, whether the game was a success or failure was likely
    used to divine answers to questions.


    <b><size=+10>Today</size></b>

    While
    Clock does have some variants which allow some level of skill, the original,
    luck-based version remains as the preferred version for many players. Whether
    playing to relax, pass some time, or simply test their luck, players continue
    to enjoy this simple game.'
  <GuideObject>k__BackingField: {fileID: 7359393755759737706, guid: e0ad9032c12a2764aa865b604b3c7951, type: 3}
